acts  upon  power  management events posted by the kernel’s power
management facility.  When  events  are  posted,  translates  the
event  into a script name and a list of arguments.  then runs the
script in order to implement the power management policy  defined
by  the  system  administrator.  The following options are avail‐
able: Enable debugging mode.  Verbose messages and  all  messages
intended  for  will be sent to stderr, and will stay in the fore‐
ground of the controlling terminal.  Prevent execution  of  power
management  scripts.   All  configuration of is encapsulated into
scripts that are run when power  management  events  occur.   The
daemon will look for the scripts from the directory Configuration
scripts are run synchronously; will start the script and wait for
its  completion  before it handles the next event.  Configuration
scripts are called with different  arguments,  depending  on  the
script  class.  These classes are described in the following sec‐
tions.  Power switch scripts are called when a state change event
occurs on a power switch device.  Power switch scripts are called
with two arguments: the device with which the  event  is  associ‐
ated,  and  the  event  type.   The following power switch script
names are defined: This script is called when an event occurs  on
a  power  button device.  This script is called when an event oc‐
curs on a reset button device.  This script  is  called  when  an
event  occurs  on  a  sleep button device.  This script is called
when an event occurs on a lid  switch  device.   This  script  is
called  when  an  online or offline event occurs on an AC adapter
device.  This script is called when an event occurs on  a  hotkey
button device.  The following events are defined for power switch
devices:  The  button  was pressed, the lid was closed, or the AC
adapter was connected.  The button  was  released,  the  lid  was
opened,  or the AC adapter was disconnected.  Note that power and
sleep button devices usually do not post this type of event.  The
following is an example of how a power button script might be in‐
voked when a power button is pressed by the  operator:  /etc/pow‐
erd/scripts/power_button acpibut0 pressed scripts are called when
a  condition was triggered in a sensor.  These scripts are called
with three arguments: the device associated, the event type,  and
the  sensor’s  name.  The and the scripts uses a fourth argument:
state description.  The following envsys  script  names  are  de‐
fined:  This  script  is called when an event occurs on a battery
sensor (Wh/Ah/Battery state).  This  script  is  called  when  an
event  occurs  on  a drive sensor.  This script is called when an
event occurs on a fan sensor.  This  script  is  called  when  an
event  occurs  on  a  indicator/integer  sensor.   This script is
called when an event occurs on a power sensor  (W/Ampere).   This
script  is  called  when  an  event occurs on a resistance sensor
(Ohm).  This script is called when an event occurs on a  tempera‐
ture  sensor.   This  script  is called when an event occurs on a
voltage sensor.  The following events are defined for fan,  indi‐
cator,  power,  resistance,  temperature,  and voltage sensors: A
critical condition was triggered.  A critical under condition was
triggered.  A critical over condition was triggered.   A  warning
under  condition  was  triggered.   A  warning over condition was
triggered.  The following event is defined for all  scripts,  but
it is only sent if any of the previous events has been previously
sent:  A normal state/capacity/condition was triggered.  The fol‐
lowing events are defined  only  for  battery  sensors:  Capacity
dropped  below  the  limit set by the user.  System is running in
low power.  This implies that the AC adapter is disconnected  and
all  batteries are in critical or low capacity.  The script shut‐
downs the system gracefully by default.  The following events are
defined for drive and battery sensors: The state  of  the  sensor
has  been changed and it is not in the normal state.  The follow‐
ing is an example of how a temperature sensor script might be in‐
voked when a critical  over  condition  is  triggered:  /etc/pow‐
